---
.title = "WebRTC: peer-to-peer for the browser",
.description = "WebRTC carries a session between two peers that cannot open a socket to each other — two browsers, or two hosts behind NATs — over an encrypted DataChannel.",
.date = @date("2026-07-22T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

WebRTC is pancake's transport for two peers that cannot open a socket to each
other: two browsers, or two hosts each behind a NAT. A session's bytes ride a
[WebRTC](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/) DataChannel, encrypted with DTLS, and the
channel opens directly between the two peers with no server on the byte path.
Everything above the wire stays the same OCapN, the object-capability network
protocol pancake speaks over TCP. What changes is how the channel comes up: a
WebRTC peer has no address to dial, so the two ends arrange the connection through
a capability they can both already reach.

## The channel comes up by signaling

To open a DataChannel, the two peers first trade three things: an SDP offer, an
SDP answer, and a handful of ICE candidates (the network paths each side can be
reached on). pancake carries that exchange over an ordinary OCapN capability. A
room both peers have joined, or any object both can send to, hosts the
signaling. The object moves opaque strings; it parses no SDP and holds no
session.

[The two peers trade an offer, an answer, and ICE candidates through a signaling capability, then open a direct DataChannel and run the keyed session over it with the signaling host out of the byte path.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-webrtc-signaling.svg'))

Once the candidates pair, the DataChannel opens directly between the two peers and
the keyed `op:start-session` handshake runs over it end to end. From that point
the signaling host is out of the byte path entirely: it saw the setup strings, not
the session, and the DTLS channel and the keyed handshake both run above it.
`zig build webrtc-signaling-captp` proves the signaling half as a real capability
over a keyed CapTP session, and `zig build webrtc-interop` proves the whole thing
between two native peers on a real DataChannel.

This is also why a bare `webrtc` locator does not dial. pancake's dial
classifier recognizes the transport and validates its designator (the peer
name inside the locator, here a public key), but with
no signaling reference there is nowhere to send an offer. A WebRTC peer is
reached through a signaling exchange the application arranges, or by redeeming
a handoff give that carries one, which the handoff section below covers.

## One key, pinned by the handshake

Like keyed TCP and iroh, a WebRTC peer is named by its Ed25519 public key in
canonical hex, not by an address. Signaling brings the channel up, and then the
keyed handshake pins the session to that designator. Whoever answers has to
prove the key the peer was introduced under, so a signaling host that swapped in
a peer of its own is refused before the session ever opens. The DTLS
fingerprint is trusted to introduce, never to impersonate, because it cannot
complete the keyed handshake for a key it does not hold.

## You only link this if you use it

Same deal as [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')): the stack is Rust, so it
ships as its own sidecar staticlib, `libpancake_net_transport_webrtc`
([ADR-0089](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0089-webrtc-datachannel-netlayer.md)),
and the base archives stay pure Zig. `zig build webrtc-abi` builds it; needs
`cargo`. Worth knowing before you commit: a native WebRTC session runs on the
blocking pump, with no completion-loop tier for DataChannels, so the
many-connections scale path stays iroh's. And the proofs on this page exist
but do not run on every push;
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) has the tier.

## The browser arm

The browser is where WebRTC earns its place. `@pancake/webrtc` drives an
`RTCPeerConnection` and presents the open DataChannel as the same byte channel the
wasm Host runs a keyed session over, the WebRTC sibling of the relay's WebSocket
channel. Two end states are proven in a real headless Chromium:

- a browser and a native peer hold a keyed session over a DataChannel
  (`zig build webrtc-browser-peer`), and
- two browsers, introduced by a signaling host, bring up a direct DataChannel and
  hold a keyed session over it with the host out of the byte path
  (`zig build webrtc-browser-to-browser`).

## STUN, TURN, and staying direct

ICE is how the two ends find a network path, and the posture mirrors iroh's:
nothing infrastructural is on by default, and a relay is a fallback, never the
steady path. On loopback and on a LAN, two peers pair on their own host
candidates with no STUN and no TURN, which is why the in-tree checks stand up
no ICE infrastructure. A browser deployment that must cross NAT hands
`@pancake/webrtc` a STUN server in its ICE server list, the way an iroh
deployment opts into discovery. The native sidecar gathers host candidates
only, so a native peer today pairs on loopback or a LAN. A TURN relay, where a
deployment configures one, carries bytes only when ICE finds no direct path at
all, and even then it sits under the DTLS channel, so it sees timing and volume
but never the keyed session.

## Three-party handoff over WebRTC

A three-party handoff is this: peer A, already talking to B, hands B a working
reference to a third peer C that B has never contacted, and B ends up talking to C
directly. pancake does it the same way on WebRTC as on TCP
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the general shape). A signs a *give*
naming C by its WebRTC designator. B redeems it by opening a DataChannel to C
through C's signaling inbox, running the keyed handshake, and presenting a
`withdraw-gift` that C's engine verifies before handing the capability over.
The result is a gifted capability reached over a channel that never had an
address.
`zig build webrtc-handoff` proves it, WebRTC's answer to `iroh-mesh`.

## See it run

Every claim here is a runnable check step (each needs `cargo`, and the browser
ones need `deno` and a Playwright Chromium):

```sh
zig build webrtc-interop            # keyed conversation between two native peers over a real DataChannel
zig build webrtc-signaling-captp    # the signaling verbs as a real OCapN capability over a keyed CapTP session
zig build webrtc-browser-peer       # a real Chromium greets a native peer over a DataChannel
zig build webrtc-browser-to-browser # two Chromium peers hold a keyed session over a direct DataChannel
zig build webrtc-handoff            # a webrtc give redeemed by signaling-then-handshake
zig build webrtc-abi                # build libpancake_net_transport_webrtc and its header
```

If you got here wondering whether you actually want
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) instead: for native peers you probably
do. The rest of the lineup is on [Transports]($link.page('transports')).
